{'match_id': 'session_2026-09-02-resea-REF-00973-MOB', 'source_ref': 'REF-00973', 'target_population': 'MOB', 'study_population': '17 people with SCI using manual wheelchairs', 'sample_size': 17, 'match_grade': 'PARTIAL', 'mismatch_note': 'SCI-only sample; does not speak for ambulant or non-SCI wheeled-mobility users.', 'created_at': '2026-09-02 21:10', 'created_by_session': 'session_2026-09-02-research-batch-05-circulation-icf', 'ref_id': 'REF-00973', 'gap_id': None}
{'match_id': 'session_2026-09-02-resea-REF-00973-SCI', 'source_ref': 'REF-00973', 'target_population': 'SCI', 'study_population': '17 people with SCI, C4-T12, AIS A/B/C, MWC >4h/day, 16 men 1 woman, mean age 39.8', 'sample_size': 17, 'match_grade': 'EXACT', 'mismatch_note': 'TWO MISMATCHES AND THE FIRST IS SEVERE. (i) Inclusion criteria require independent community mobility in the MWC INCLUDING ASCENDING AN ACCESS RAMP, so the study structurally SELECTS OUT exactly the people for whom ramps are already impassable; the finding is a lower bound on population burden. (ii) 16 of 17 participants are men, and shoulder anthropometry and strength are sexually dimorphic, so this cannot ground a claim about women wheelchair users.', 'created_at': '2026-09-02 21:10', 'created_by_session': 'session_2026-09-02-research-batch-05-circulation-icf', 'ref_id': 'REF-00973', 'gap_id': None}
{'match_id': 'session_2026-09-02-resea-REF-00974-MOB', 'source_ref': 'REF-00974', 'target_population': 'MOB', 'study_population': 'ten able-bodied participants', 'sample_size': 10, 'match_grade': 'PROXY', 'mismatch_note': 'Stated plainly: NO WHEELCHAIR USER TOOK PART. Able-bodied participants have intact trunk control, no shoulder overuse history, no SCI-related muscle imbalance and no chronic exposure - they lack the very condition the study exists to inform. The absolute forces are indicative only; the RANKING across activities is the transferable part, and even that is a hypothesis for real users.', 'created_at': '2026-09-02 21:10', 'created_by_session': 'session_2026-09-02-research-batch-05-circulation-icf', 'ref_id': 'REF-00974', 'gap_id': None}
{'match_id': 'session_2026-09-02-resea-REF-00975-MOB', 'source_ref': 'REF-00975', 'target_population': 'MOB', 'study_population': '145 respondents: 112 people with mobility impairment plus 33 family members', 'sample_size': 145, 'match_grade': 'PARTIAL', 'mismatch_note': 'TWO WRITTEN MISMATCHES. (i) 22.7 percent of responses are PROXY VOICE - a carer or relative answering on behalf of the disabled person; under a Co-1 reading that alone would disqualify, under T3 it is a stated limitation. (ii) Australia-only, over-represents severe and profound impairment against a 2.9 percent population base rate, and over-represents under-65s. NOT graded for BLIND, DEAF, AUT, ID or DEM: those populations were not sampled.', 'created_at': '2026-09-02 21:10', 'created_by_session': 'session_2026-09-02-research-batch-05-circulation-icf', 'ref_id': 'REF-00975', 'gap_id': None}
{'match_id': 'session_2026-09-02-resea-REF-00975-PAIN', 'source_ref': 'REF-00975', 'target_population': 'PAIN', 'study_population': 'arthritis 28.9pc, back problems 28.3pc, knee problems 24.1pc', 'sample_size': 145, 'match_grade': 'PARTIAL', 'mismatch_note': 'Pain conditions are the most common reported impairments but the study measures housing accessibility, not pain response; the crossing is inferential.', 'created_at': '2026-09-02 21:10', 'created_by_session': 'session_2026-09-02-research-batch-05-circulation-icf', 'ref_id': 'REF-00975', 'gap_id': None}
{'match_id': 'session_2026-09-02-resea-REF-00976-MOB', 'source_ref': 'REF-00976', 'target_population': 'MOB', 'study_population': 'field survey of newly built Japanese residences - dwellings, not people', 'sample_size': 0, 'match_grade': 'PROXY', 'mismatch_note': 'The unit of analysis is a BUILDING, not a person. Wheelchair movement is assessed as a geometric possibility, never observed. Japanese dwelling stock and Japanese body and device dimensions may not transfer. Grade is provisional on the English abstract only, per R15.', 'created_at': '2026-09-02 21:10', 'created_by_session': 'session_2026-09-02-research-batch-05-circulation-icf', 'ref_id': 'REF-00976', 'gap_id': None}
{'match_id': 'session_2026-09-02-resea-REF-00977-MOB', 'source_ref': 'REF-00977', 'target_population': 'MOB', 'study_population': 'synthesis of 48 studies of mobility assistive device users', 'sample_size': 48, 'match_grade': 'PARTIAL', 'mismatch_note': 'The review states its own limitation: the reviewed studies mostly focused on wheelchair users residing in high-income countries. The synthesis therefore inherits a high-income, wheelchair-centric skew and does not speak for LMB, LPA, BAR or the Global South.', 'created_at': '2026-09-02 21:10', 'created_by_session': 'session_2026-09-02-research-batch-05-circulation-icf', 'ref_id': 'REF-00977', 'gap_id': None}
{'match_id': 'session_2026-09-02-resea-REF-00978-ALL', 'source_ref': 'REF-00978', 'target_population': 'ALL', 'study_population': 'UK-wide self-selecting online survey, over 4,400 respondents. Q1 (p.11), multi-select: a disabled person 88%, family member or friend 13%, unpaid or family carer 9%, carer or PA 2%, someone of retirement age 11%, teacher/social worker/healthcare professional 1%, someone else 1%.', 'sample_size': 4400, 'match_grade': 'PARTIAL', 'mismatch_note': "THE SURVEY ASKS NO IMPAIRMENT-TYPE QUESTION, so no population is separable from the sample and every percentage is whole-sample. That is why the target is ALL and the grade is PARTIAL rather than EXACT: the source can say that 56% of a large disabled sample commonly meet in-building circulation barriers, and cannot say which populations carry that 56%. Roughly 12% of responses come from proxies (family, carer, PA, professional) rather than disabled people themselves, and the instrument does not let proxy responses be excluded from the barrier percentages. Self-selection through a disabled-access charity's own channels biases toward people who already encounter and report access barriers; the direction inflates prevalence and does not affect the RANKING of barriers against each other, which is what this source is admitted for.", 'created_at': '2026-09-02 21:34', 'created_by_session': 'session_2026-09-02-research-batch-05-circulation-icf', 'ref_id': 'REF-00978', 'gap_id': None}
{'match_id': 'session_2026-09-02-resea-REF-00978-MOB', 'source_ref': 'REF-00978', 'target_population': 'MOB', 'study_population': 'Mobility-device use is reported only indirectly, via Q29 main modes of transport (p.22), multi-select: wheelchair 25%, mobility scooter 24%, powerchair 15%, wheelchair with a power attachment 6%, walking 12%, car owned or leased 91%.', 'sample_size': 4400, 'match_grade': 'PARTIAL', 'mismatch_note': 'Q29 is a transport question, not an impairment question, and it is NOT cross-tabulated against the Q7 barriers question. So mobility-device users are demonstrably a large share of the sample, but the 56% could-not-get-around-the-venue figure cannot be attributed to them specifically - it is the whole-sample figure. Graded PARTIAL for that reason. Using this source to anchor a MOB-specific value would assert a cross-tabulation the report does not publish.', 'created_at': '2026-09-02 21:34', 'created_by_session': 'session_2026-09-02-research-batch-05-circulation-icf', 'ref_id': 'REF-00978', 'gap_id': None}
{'match_id': 'session_2026-09-02-resea-REF-00978-MOB-2', 'source_ref': 'REF-00978', 'target_population': 'MOB', 'study_population': 'DISSENTING SECOND ROW (adversarial pass, 2026-09-02). Same sample as the PARTIAL row on this ref; the disagreement is about what the sample can carry, not about who was in it.', 'sample_size': 4400, 'match_grade': 'PROXY', 'mismatch_note': "The PARTIAL row on this ref asserts that self-selection 'does not affect the RANKING of barriers against each other, which is what this source is admitted for.' That claim is unevidenced and is most likely wrong in a specific direction. On the face of the report: the survey is supported by the Motability Scheme (p.3), Motability Operations supplies two of three pull-quotes and an About page (pp.9-10), Q29 reports 'Car - owned or leased' at 91 percent (p.22), and the top-ranked barrier at Q7 is 'A lack of appropriate parking available' at 64 percent (p.14) - the sponsor's own domain. A sample that is 91 percent car-owning, recruited through a disabled-access charity co-branded with a car-lease scheme, is precisely the recruitment that would inflate the parking option relative to the others. The report publishes NO methodology - no sampling method, no fieldwork dates, no per-question base, no weighting anywhere in 29 pages - so sponsor-channel recruitment can be neither confirmed nor excluded, which is why this is graded PROXY rather than left implicit. WHAT SURVIVES: the in-building comparison this source is actually used for - could not get around the venue 56 percent against could not get into the venue 41 percent and no suitable toilet 41 percent - is between three options none of which is the sponsor's domain, and is not touched by the parking argument. WHAT DOES NOT: any use of the whole-question ranking, including any statement that parking is the leading barrier for disabled people.", 'created_at': '2026-09-02 21:51', 'created_by_session': 'session_2026-09-02-research-batch-05-circulation-icf', 'ref_id': 'REF-00978', 'gap_id': None}
{'match_id': 'session_2026-09-10-resea-REF-00979-MOB', 'source_ref': 'REF-00979', 'target_population': 'MOB', 'study_population': 'Young men (N=10) with paraplegia; repeated-measures, wooden ramp 7.3 m, slopes 0-12 degrees, biomechanics laboratory (PMID 19236980 abstract)', 'sample_size': 10, 'match_grade': 'PARTIAL', 'mismatch_note': 'Male-only, young-only, paraplegia-only, N=10. No powerchair or scooter users, no women, no older adults. MOB spans mobility-aid users this sample does not represent, and the 4-10 degree adjustment band it reports is a manual-wheelchair upslope finding.', 'created_at': '2026-09-11 04:50', 'created_by_session': 'session_2026-09-10-research-batch-06-circulation-geometry', 'ref_id': 'REF-00979', 'gap_id': None}
{'match_id': 'session_2026-09-10-resea-REF-00979-SCI', 'source_ref': 'REF-00979', 'target_population': 'SCI', 'study_population': 'Young men (N=10) with paraplegia; aetiology NOT reported (PMID 19236980 abstract)', 'sample_size': 10, 'match_grade': 'PARTIAL', 'mismatch_note': 'The source states PARAPLEGIA, not spinal cord injury, and PubMed indexes it under the Paraplegia descriptor with no Spinal Cord Injuries term. Paraplegia also follows spina bifida, transverse myelitis and tumour, so an SCI reading is inferred from presentation rather than stated. Compare REF-00973, graded EXACT on SCI because it reports C4-T12 AIS A/B/C explicitly.', 'created_at': '2026-09-11 04:50', 'created_by_session': 'session_2026-09-10-research-batch-06-circulation-geometry', 'ref_id': 'REF-00979', 'gap_id': None}
{'match_id': 'session_2026-09-10-resea-REF-00980-MOB', 'source_ref': 'REF-00980', 'target_population': 'MOB', 'study_population': '171 subjects of all ages using different types of mobility aids, traversing a 30-foot ramp at slopes from 1:8 to 1:20 (PMID 10168021 abstract)', 'sample_size': 171, 'match_grade': 'EXACT', 'mismatch_note': 'Graded from the abstract; the full text was not read. The abstract states all ages and different types of mobility aids but does not enumerate the device types or their proportions, so the breadth is asserted by the source rather than shown. Its own finding is stratified -- only a few MANUAL wheelchair users had difficulty at 1:8 -- so device type is load-bearing for any value drawn from it.', 'created_at': '2026-09-11 04:50', 'created_by_session': 'session_2026-09-10-research-batch-06-circulation-geometry', 'ref_id': 'REF-00980', 'gap_id': None}

